{# Methodology section #}
<div class="section" id="methodology">
  <h2>Methodology</h2>
  <div class="methodology">
    <p><strong>Distance calculation:</strong> Haversine formula for straight-line distances between
    consecutive stops. Road-network distances require an OSRM matrix provider.</p>

    <p><strong>Sequencing analysis:</strong> Compares actual stop order against nearest-neighbor
    heuristic. Routes with a sequencing index above the configured threshold are flagged.</p>

    <p><strong>Time modeling:</strong> Includes pre-trip inspection, drive time (based on distance
    and assumed speed), service time per stop, idle/wait time at time windows, lunch break
    insertion after the configured threshold, and post-trip time.</p>

    {% set traffic = metadata.get('traffic_profile', {}) if metadata else {} %}
    {% if traffic.get('active') %}
    <p><strong>Travel times:</strong> Road-network travel times are <em>free-flow</em> estimates
    from OSRM, adjusted by the configured traffic profile. They are <strong>not</strong> live or
    historical traffic data. Each leg is scaled by the speed factor of the band containing the
    vehicle's estimated departure time from that leg's origin:</p>
    <table class="metrics-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Window (local)</th><th>Speed factor</th><th>Effect on travel time</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for band in traffic.get('bands', []) %}
        <tr>
          <td>{{ band.start }}&ndash;{{ band.end }}</td>
          <td>{{ '%.2f' | format(band.speed_factor) }}&times;</td>
          <td>{{ '%+.0f' | format((1.0 / band.speed_factor - 1.0) * 100) }}%</td>
        </tr>
        {% endfor %}
        <tr>
          <td>All other times</td>
          <td>{{ '%.2f' | format(traffic.get('default_factor', 1.0)) }}&times;</td>
          <td>{{ '%+.0f' | format((1.0 / traffic.get('default_factor', 1.0) - 1.0) * 100) }}%</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p><strong>Band assignment (single-pass approximation):</strong> Each leg's band is assigned
    from the plan's estimated departure times, propagated from the planned start time through
    free-flow travel and service time. Because applying the profile lengthens travel, the true
    departure times shift later than the free-flow estimates used to assign the bands; this pass
    is deliberately <em>not</em> iterated to a fixed point. Legs departing near a band boundary
    may therefore be assigned the adjacent band, and a leg is banded by its departure time only
    &mdash; a leg that starts before a peak band and runs into it is not split.</p>
    <p>Plan grading and the benchmark optimum are computed on this same adjusted matrix, so the
    comparison between them is internally consistent. Distances are unaffected by the profile.</p>
    {% if benchmark and benchmark.fleet_level %}
    <p><strong>Fleet-level banding:</strong> In the cross-route (VRPTW) benchmark, each stop is
    banded by its own route's schedule. The depot is shared by every vehicle, but each route has
    its own start time and the solver costs each leg once regardless of which vehicle drives it,
    so a single factor is applied to all depot departures &mdash; taken from the median route
    start time. This affects only the depot-to-first-stop leg of routes starting in a different
    band from the median.</p>
    {% endif %}
    {% else %}
    <p><strong>Travel times:</strong> Road-network travel times are <em>free-flow</em> estimates
    from OSRM, based on static road attributes with no time-of-day variation. No traffic profile
    was applied. They are <strong>not</strong> live or historical traffic data.</p>
    <p>Comparative results (plan vs. optimal, route vs. fleet median) largely cancel this bias,
    since both sides share the same clock. Absolute results do not: time-window compliance and
    shift-overrun minutes are computed against optimistic travel times, so violations and
    overruns should be read as <strong>lower bounds</strong>. Congestion can only push arrivals
    later, never earlier.</p>
    {% endif %}

    <p><strong>Benchmark optimization:</strong> Uses Google OR-Tools TSPTW solver for single-route
    optimization and VRPTW solver for fleet-level optimization.</p>

    <p><strong>What the benchmark does and does not prove:</strong> Both solvers run a
    time-limited metaheuristic (guided local search). What they return is the
    <strong>best solution found within the time limit</strong> — <em>not</em> a proven optimum,
    and not a proven bound on the distance to one. A reported gap therefore measures how much
    the solver improved on the plan, not how wasteful the plan provably is.</p>

    <p>The practical consequence is that <strong>reported gaps are conservative</strong>: the
    true recoverable waste is <em>at least</em> the figure shown, and may be more, because a
    better solution may exist that the solver did not find. A gap of zero or below means only
    that the solver found nothing better than the plan within its budget — the plan is within
    solver reach. It does not prove the plan is optimal.</p>

    <p><strong>Territory analysis:</strong> Detects geographic overlap between routes using convex
    hull intersection and point-in-polygon tests. Depot stress measured via haversine distance.</p>

    <p><strong>Findings classification:</strong> All findings are deterministic and reproducible.
    Severity is assigned based on configurable thresholds. Finding IDs are computed via
    SHA-256 hash of (category, references, evidence) for deduplication.</p>

    {% if analyses_run %}
    <p><strong>Analyses run:</strong> {{ analyses_run | join(', ') }}</p>
    {% endif %}
    {% if analyses_skipped %}
    <p><strong>Analyses skipped:</strong>
      {% for name, reason in analyses_skipped %}{{ name }} ({{ reason }}){% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}
    </p>
    {% endif %}
  </div>
</div>
